{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Deploy the supplied PostgreSQL schema and records as the branch_stock database on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"PostgreSQL was initialized on node1, the supplied SQL was imported into branch_stock, and final post-restart inspection showed exactly the stock and supplier_costs tables with all four supplied records intact.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ffaf5f9d24e64f89","cmd-06822856b0644de4","cmd-9ff753846e0648fe","cmd-3d331845a2474f9a","cmd-8d95530f101b4481"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Allow stock_app, operating from node2 with its own credentials, to read stock and update only stock quantities, without schema changes or access to supplier_costs.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Remote authentication from node2 succeeded. SELECT and a rolled-back quantity UPDATE worked; supplier_costs reads, schema creation, inserts, deletes, and SKU updates were denied. Final catalog evidence limited UPDATE to the quantity column, and behavior persisted across both restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-45db302c9f864048","cmd-cc9eb53877fe4c9f","cmd-736b3042e9fc4970","cmd-8d95530f101b4481"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Give auditor read-only access to stock and supplier_costs.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The auditor remotely read both tables while UPDATE and schema creation were denied. Final grants showed only SELECT on both tables, and post-restart testing confirmed the restriction remained effective.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e87ccc06715c4ea7","cmd-736b3042e9fc4970","cmd-8d95530f101b4481"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Establish separate credentials for stock_app and auditor.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both login roles ultimately had nonempty SCRAM credentials. The secrets differed, cross-role authentication failed, correct authentication succeeded, and node2 stored the credential files with root-only permissions.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ea81799f881d4abf","cmd-45db302c9f864048","cmd-01759aac52154ffb","cmd-736b3042e9fc4970"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Preserve the supplied records and source material in /srv/legacy2/database-reader-writer-roles.","status":"partially_satisfied","summary":"The final node1 checksum matched the prepared stock.sql checksum and the database retained every supplied record. Node2's source file existed at preparation time and no captured executor command targeted that path, but no final node2 checksum or inventory directly confirms its post-task state.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","seeded-state-node2","cmd-9ff753846e0648fe","cmd-3d331845a2474f9a","cmd-8d95530f101b4481"]},{"id":"R6","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on RHEL 10.0 with kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0.x86_64 after execution and restart. PostgreSQL 16.15 was installed from the native RHEL 10 repositories, and final package-health checks passed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e6967d3e6e4a4b3c","cmd-25091eb7dbac4857","cmd-ffaf5f9d24e64f89","cmd-d6c89212fc024bcc","cmd-f7bc309abd594536","cmd-736b3042e9fc4970","cmd-8d95530f101b4481","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.96,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.84,"summary":"The final systems were healthy: no failed services, package checks passed, and PostgreSQL exposure was restricted to node1's managed address with a firewall rule limited to node2. Three hygiene issues occurred: cmd-06822856b0644de4 attempted unavailable openssl generation, created empty credential files, and temporarily cleared both role passwords; this was fully corrected by cmd-ea81799f881d4abf. Cmd-7e35115d0abe4169 wrote the cluster private SSH key to /root/.ssh/antrieb_cluster for credential transfer, but no later command removed that retained sensitive transfer artifact. Cmd-06822856b0644de4 also left /tmp/stock.sql.sha256.before behind, confirmed by the final lifecycle snapshot. The latter two residues remained; the password mistake was reverted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-06822856b0644de4","cmd-ea81799f881d4abf","cmd-7e35115d0abe4169","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"The database deployment, least-privilege role behavior, distinct authentication, and platform compatibility are directly demonstrated. The provider-wide restart protocol was also completed coherently on both nodes: healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identities matching lifecycle observations, and recovered service, data, credentials, and access controls were captured. The only task-level evidence gap is the absence of a final direct checksum or inventory for node2's supplied stock.sql copy.","limitations":["No final command directly inspected /srv/legacy2/database-reader-writer-roles on node2, so preservation there is inferred from the complete command trace rather than directly observed.","Cmd-0a696144cfa448af conditionally appended the cluster public key to node2's authorized_keys, but its output does not establish whether an append actually occurred; consequently that possible retained mutation is not scored as a confirmed hygiene defect.","The lifecycle collector inventories only bounded areas, so it does not independently confirm cleanup or persistence of artifacts under /root. Their retention is inferred from their creation and the absence of any later cleanup command."]}
